\section{Preserve the C Declaration Until Target Selection}

A foreign declaration describes how to find a function and which
native declaration is authoritative. It should not just rename an
ordinary source type. Consider a statistics library that exports
\texttt{int stats\_add(long sample, size\_t weight)}.
If the host has arbitrary-precision integers, both parameters can be
ordinary integers at the public surface, but they have distinct C
types and conversion rules. A negative sample is legitimate. A
negative weight is not made legitimate by reduction modulo a power of
two. A too-large sample must be rejected before the call unless this
particular wrapper deliberately specifies a different operation.

The scalar table in the local System V AMD64 supplement gives
eight-byte sizes and alignment for \texttt{long} and pointers in the
model it describes. This is LP64: C \texttt{int} remains 32 bits.
A common Windows x64 configuration instead uses LLP64: pointers are
wide but C \texttt{long} remains 32 bits. This distinction is
independent of the register-passing rules. A declaration mapping
keyed only by the word ``x64'' cannot handle even these two models,
let alone a target with 32-bit pointers on the same instruction set.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrp{0.35\linewidth}}
\hline
C type & LP64 bits & LLP64 bits & Meaning in the binding \\
\hline
\texttt{int} & 32 & 32 & Signed C integer \\
\texttt{long} & 64 & 32 & Signed C integer \\
\texttt{size\_t} & 64 & 64 & Unsigned size in these targets \\
\texttt{void *} & 64 & 64 & Object pointer, not a size \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The table describes the two data models used in this example, not a
portable recipe for deducing the layout of every typedef from the name
of a data model. Obtain the target's facts from the same toolchain and
configuration that see the library header. Where the source identifier
is a macro or is only defined as a static inline helper, there may be
no exported symbol to load; generate a C shim compiled with the header
instead of trying to resolve the spelling of the helper at runtime.

\section{Keep Representation and Permission Separate}

Represent a resolved foreign parameter as three pieces:
source-language type, target ABI type and a conversion or access rule.
A function result that is a nullable opaque pointer and an argument
that is a writable buffer may use the same pointer representation and
be passed by the same register class. A caller cannot substitute one
for the other. Preserve pointee kind, nullability, lifetime and extent
in the language-facing descriptor even when machine lowering discards
those distinctions. Furr and Foster's representational types for
OCaml and Java make a related point: knowing the low-level C type
does not by itself say which high-level operations are valid on a
value represented that way.

The source declaration may expose distinct wrappers for signed and
unsigned native parameters that have the same width. Read an unsigned
64-bit return into a host integer without treating the top bit as a
sign bit. For a C enumeration, retain named constants and determine
the native representation under the actual compiler flags; decide
what to do with unknown values before declaring the language enum
exhaustive. For plain \texttt{char}, retain the distinction from
\texttt{signed char} and \texttt{unsigned char}. A character code
unit and a Unicode scalar are distinct even when both appear on the
language side as integers.

\Cref{fig:visual-386} splits two consumers of the same declaration.
The stub emitter receives a machine signature; the wrapper emitter
receives mapping rules whose details should survive all the way to
diagnostics. Conflating them makes it difficult to explain why a
particular language value is ill-formed for a valid native signature.

