% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Elaborate a Declaration and Apply Its Checks}

\begin{verbatim}
elaborate(decl, target):
    require(supported_convention(decl.convention, target))
    signature := []
    conversions := []
    for parameter in decl.parameters:
        c_type := resolve_typedefs(parameter.c_type, target)
        signature.append(abi_representation(c_type, target))
        conversions.append(converter(parameter.policy, c_type))
    result := resolve_typedefs(decl.result.c_type, target)
    return BoundDecl(decl.symbol, decl.convention, signature,
                     abi_representation(result, target),
                     conversions, decoder(decl.result.policy, result))

checked_integer(value, c_type):
    require(is_host_integer(value))
    bits := c_type.value_bits_including_sign
    if c_type.is_signed:
        lo := -(2 ** (bits - 1))
        hi :=  (2 ** (bits - 1)) - 1
    else:
        lo := 0
        hi := (2 ** bits) - 1
    require(lo <= value <= hi)
    return encode_native_integer(value, c_type)
\end{verbatim}

The integer helper describes an ordinary two's-complement target and
uses an arithmetic domain that can represent both bounds. Do not
compute \(2^{64}\) with a machine operation that cannot represent it.
Boolean parameters are mapped by a different rule: convert the
language truth value to the native representation explicitly, not
through a test based on unspecified upper register bits. Likewise
support for native floating point must specify both rounding and
exceptional values; a host real that cannot be converted to a finite
\texttt{float} should not silently become an infinity if the API
requires finite inputs.

\section{Use a Small Value Matrix to Expose Width Drift}

For the example, generate cases at both ends of the 32-bit signed
range and just outside it. On the LLP64 target the wrapper rejects
the outside samples; on LP64 it can pass them. Pass \(2^{63}\) as an
unsigned weight to both, if the library's semantic range permits it,
and check a native fixture receives that exact value. Also pass
\(-1\) as a weight and ensure the native function was never entered.
A test suite using only \(0\), \(1\), and \(-1\) as signed samples
cannot detect the mapping error. Keep tests of conversion validity
separate from tests of argument delivery: the correct language range
paired with an incorrectly generated machine signature is still wrong.

\section{Elaborate Aliases Without Erasing Their Boundary Meaning}

Imagine a header introduces two aliases using the same unsigned
integer storage: one for a byte count, another for a sample
number. A front end can share the native type node for both,
but should preserve their names in source locations and allow
their binding policies to differ. For instance, an arbitrary
sample number can fit the native width and still refer to no
sample; a byte count of that width can exceed the extent of
the allocation paired with it. Validate the latter relation
at the call after argument conversion, rather than defining
all values of the native type as an acceptable buffer length.

For an example spanning preprocessing, suppose a library
publishes a count alias that is 32 bits unless an extended
mode is enabled. Both variants have the same function names,
so export lookup cannot tell which was compiled. Generate the
raw declaration from the exact compiler configuration and
compare a supported library version or configuration query
before using the interface. Keep the alias spelling in the
error report: an announcement that ``unsigned integer size
disagrees'' is less actionable than identifying which count
typedef changed and under which define. If no metadata or
deployment rule can establish that the library matches the
header, choose a provider-supplied shim or refuse to guess.

Incomplete types offer a different example of intentional
information loss. The generator can accept a pointer to an
incomplete type as an opaque argument without knowing the
object size. It should refuse a request to allocate the
pointee or embed it by value. The same principle is useful
for unmodeled types: representation knowledge should be
required only for the operations the binding actually offers.
